The commands that turn a booted machine into an NVKE node, run before kubeadm.
Rendered by BuildForgeNodePayloads, one command per non-blank line, and sent as
NodePoolCreateConfig.infra.preKubeadmCommands.

The package versions are inputs for the same reason the OS image is: a
qualification run exists to pin them.

Every NVKE package installed here comes from the image's own apt repository,
the flat file: repo that os-image/packer/scripts/provision.sh stages under
/usr/lib/nvke/debs and indexes at bake time. No NVKE apt source is configured
on the node, so what it installs is what the image carries, and the versions
below have to be ones that image locks. A version it does not carry resolves to
no candidate and fails here. That is the intended answer: a node reaching a
repository for these would install whichever build the suite currently holds,
which is a different build than the one under qualification, and nothing in the
run would say so.

Both OVN packages, not just -images. The agent resolves the charts package
name and requires it to already be installed: chartsDebPresenceCheck returns
errCNIChartsDebNotInstalled and the agent never installs it itself. That
failure terminates the provisioning workflow rather than retrying, so putting
the package on afterwards does not recover the run. openvswitch-switch is here
for the same reason — OVN runs Open vSwitch on the host, and the agent checks
for ovs-vsctl before helm. Both were observed on real Forge hardware before
they were read out of the agent source.

The image installs Open vSwitch at bake time, so this install is a no-op that
asserts it is there. Older images carry Ubuntu's openvswitch-switch; current
ones carry DOCA's doca-openvswitch-switch, which Provides it, and apt
selects that as the only provider. --no-upgrade keeps it a no-op: without it an
install can pull a newer Open vSwitch than the one the image was baked and
smoke-tested with, which is the same defect this file fixes for the NVKE
packages.

nke-agent and spire-agent are deliberately absent. The image builds both as
.debs from this checkout's source at bake time and installs them with dpkg, so
their versions are the checkout's `git describe` and they are the builds under
qualification (os-image/AGENTS.md, os-image/SPEC.md). spire-agent also owns the
systemd units, the first-boot renderer and the TPM plugin checksum that
agent.conf resolves against. A node without a baked agent is the
bring-your-own-Ubuntu path, which bootstraps with --disable-mtls and is out of
scope here.

The unit and its environment file stay nke-, permanently. nvke-agent.service
declares Alias=nke-agent.service and reads both environment files, so the old
spelling works on images built before and after #1565 and the new one only
after. Do not "finish" this rename.

set -eu is the first command for the reason the site node has it
(internal/infra/provision/userdata.go): cloud-init runs these as one /bin/sh
script and reports the exit status of the last one, which is the
touch at the end. Without the guard a failed apt step still writes the
success sentinel, so the node reports a clean cloud-init while missing the
packages it needs to join, never phones home, and the run spends its whole
budget on KubernetesReady=Unknown ("Node data not available") with nothing
saying why. set -u is safe because every command below is fully rendered
before it is sent: none of them reads a shell variable.

There is no apt-get update. os-image/packer/scripts/provision.sh indexes the
local repo at bake time and clears the Ubuntu archive lists, precisely so
"cluster create needs no apt-get update of its own". Running one here would
refresh the archive again and let the Open vSwitch install upgrade past
the baked build, which is the defect this file fixes for the NVKE packages.

The route wait stays, because nke-agent.service is restarted at the end of
this script and has to reach the controller. Forge hosts race cloud-init for
the network, so on a node whose default route is not up yet that restart comes
before the node can phone home. The amd64 kernel line in nico_forge_ipxe.tmpl
does not carry ip=dhcp the way the arm64 one does, so amd64 nodes reach this
point earlier relative to their network. Observed as an amd64 control plane
failing cloud-init while an arm64 worker running these same commands passed,
and the amd64 site node beside them passed only because its own user data
waits for a route first. Bounded, so a node that never gets a route fails here
with a line saying so rather than hanging the boot.
*/ -}}
set -eu
timeout 300s sh -c 'until ip -4 route list default | grep -q .; do echo "waiting for an IPv4 default route"; sleep 5; done'
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y nvke-kubeadm-distro-{{ .KubernetesVersion }}
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y nvke-cni-ovn-images-{{ .CNIVersion }} nvke-cni-ovn-charts-{{ .CNIVersion }}
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-upgrade openvswitch-switch
printf 'AGENT_ARGS="{{ .AgentArgs }}"\n' > /etc/default/nke-agent
sysctl --system
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart nke-agent.service
systemctl restart procps
touch /run/cloud-init.done
